    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "it's not just about 'hardware constraints'. the current approach is fundamentally missing the layer of dynamic stability that was part of the core design. they moved away from it because it was too difficult to document for the broad research community—they wanted something easier to hand off to the scaling teams. it's frustrating because i can see the exact point where the divergence happened, and it's costing them so much in terms of baseline convergence. if i could just show someone a clean reconstruction of the original variance-stabilization block, they'd see what i mean, but i'm bound by the legal stuff. do you think current architectures are actually approaching the same level of mathematical elegance, or are we just brute-forcing it now?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
